  "title": "Mamba: Linear-Time Sequence Modeling with Selective State Spaces",
  "text": "**What it is** – Mamba is a linear‑time sequence‑modeling architecture that replaces the Transformer’s quadratic‑cost attention with *selective state‑space models* (SSMs) whose parameters can change depending on the current input, allowing it to handle very long sequences efficiently [2312.00752|abstract].\n\n**The idea** –  \n1. **Selection mechanism** – Traditional SSMs use fixed (time‑invariant) parameters, which limits their ability to focus on or ignore particular tokens. Mamba makes the SSM parameters a function of the input token, so the model can *selectively* propagate useful information and forget irrelevant data on the fly [2312.00752|introduction‑selection Mechanism].  \n2. **Hardware‑aware recurrent algorithm** – Because input‑dependent parameters break the usual convolution‑based fast computation, the authors devise a scan‑style recurrent implementation that keeps the expanded state in fast GPU memory (SRAM) and avoids costly memory transfers, preserving linear‑time scaling while being up to 3× faster on A100 GPUs [2312.00752|introduction‑Hardware‑aware Algorithm].  \n3. **Simplified architecture** – They merge the SSM block with the MLP block of Transformers into a single homogeneous layer (the “Mamba” block), removing explicit attention or separate MLP layers. The resulting model is fully recurrent, scales linearly in sequence length during training, and needs only constant time per step at inference because it does not cache previous states [2312.00752|introduction‑Architecture].\n\n**Why it gets cited** – Subsequent papers refer to Mamba as a state‑space model that achieves strong performance on long‑sequence tasks with lower compute and memory than Transformers. For example, a watermarking work cites Mamba as an efficient SSM backbone and notes that its “forget‑gate mechanism and modified block design” are key to its effectiveness [2608.20580|model]. A broader survey of temporal models also lists Mamba alongside other diagonal SSMs (HiPPO, S4, etc.) as a representative linear‑time recurrent approach [2608.24051|related work].\n\n**In simpler terms** – Imagine reading a long book with a notebook that automatically decides, for each new sentence, whether to write it down, erase it, or keep it forever, based on what the sentence says. Traditional models either copy every sentence (quadratic cost) or use a fixed rule that can’t adapt. Mamba’s notebook (the selective state‑space) looks at each sentence, decides on the spot what to remember, and does all this with a fast, streamlined pen that never slows down, even for a million‑page book. This lets it understand very long texts as well as Transformers, but without the heavy “look‑everywhere” cost."
